1	// THE REPAIR PHASE of the scheduler run (docs/PRINCIPLES.md) — the recovery that
2	// used to run once a day as a task of its own, planned here as ops the same run
3	// applies before it asks any task whether it wants to run.
4	//
5	// IT IS STILL A FALLBACK. Every rule below repairs something that already went
6	// wrong — a label swap that tore, a session that died, a park nobody answered, a
7	// terminal nobody closed — and the healthy flow of a task never passes through
8	// here: an item the machinery handled correctly is settled by whoever handled it,
9	// before any of this runs. A new rule here is a claim that a failure mode exists
10	// and that nothing nearer to it can close it out; the alternative to writing one
11	// is usually fixing the flow that left the mess. What changed when this moved is
12	// its siting and its latency, not its standing.
13	//
14	// AND IT ONLY EVER READS OPEN ITEMS. An item somebody closed is finished, park
15	// label and all: a person ending a park by closing its issue is an answer, not a
16	// state to repair, and nothing here reopens, re-labels or re-nags one.
17	//
18	// WHY IT RUNS BEFORE THE ASK. Repair frees what the ask then reads: closing an
19	// abandoned failure park releases the task's lane, and the occurrence the task is
20	// owed is filed in the same run rather than one tick later. That ordering is the
21	// whole reason this is a phase of the run and not a task behind it — a task's own
22	// repair could only ever land after the tick that filed it, and could never feed
23	// the drain gate that dispatches an executor for what it freed.
24	//
25	// The verdicts stay pure in `./repair-rules.mjs`; this module decides
26	// which op each verdict becomes and which of them may be threaded back into the
27	// item list the ask reads. The shell in `run.mjs` does the GitHub I/O.
